1.5  How a program starts: fork and exec

TL;DR
  - A new process starts as a copy of an existing one: fork() duplicates the
    process that calls it.
  - exec() then replaces the copy's program with another: the kernel loads the
    new ELF file and its loader (17.2).
  - Your shell does both for every command you type. Every process descends from
    PID 1.

      pstree -p $$       # your shell and what it started
      strace -f -e trace=clone,clone3,execve bash -c 'ls; true'

On your machine
  Kernel    6.12.48-amd64, built with gcc 14.2.0
  Build     #1 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Debian 6.12.48-1 (2025-09-20)
  Work      in the 3 days, 4 h since boot: 912.3 million context switches (3,330
            per second), 1.2 million processes started, 51.2 million interrupts
  Now       3 tasks running or ready, 1 waiting on I/O
  Disk FS   ext4, vfat, btrfs

Pitfalls
  - fork copies no memory up front: pages are shared until one side writes to
    them (copy-on-write), so forking even a big process is cheap.
  - After exec, the PID stays the same: it's the same process running another
    program.
  - Environment variables and open files survive exec: that's how export and
    redirections reach the program (20.3).
  - A parent that never waits for its children leaves zombies (10.1).

Deep dive

  ▾ Why two steps instead of one?

    Between fork and exec, the child still runs the parent's code, so it can
    prepare its own setup: open a file as its output, change folder, drop
    privileges, set limits. Then it becomes the new program, which inherits all
    of that. That's how the shell handles cmd > file without cmd knowing
    anything about it.

  ▾ What does the kernel do in exec?

    It reads the start of the file. For an ELF program, it builds a fresh
    address space, maps the program's code and data into it, also maps the
    dynamic loader when the program needs one, puts the arguments and the
    environment on a new stack, and jumps to the entry point. For a file
    starting with #!, it runs the interpreter named there instead.

